commit f94ba7211659d15544100e378870560c39cd08ce
Author: Mike Holownych <mike@holownych.com>
Date:   Thu Nov 5 18:50:06 2026 +0000

    fix(step): pause --step at one shared cycle boundary after endStep

    The step pause sat in two places (after the sequential tool loop and after
    the parallel-read batch) and broke out of the loop before endStep(). The
    sequential copy also had no `!stopReason` guard, so a STAGNATION_DETECTED
    set during the cycle (recovery exhausted, repetition stop) was replaced by
    STEP_PAUSED. Skipping endStep() meant the pending recovery note was never
    delivered and tracker.evaluateStep never ran, so stepsWithoutProgress could
    not advance across /step calls and a stuck objective stepped forever.

    Both copies are removed. The pause is now one check at the loop top, after
    the stop, budget, stagnation and cancel checks: every path that continues
    the loop finishes its cycle with endStep() and comes back there, so the
    sequential, parallel-read and notice/nudge paths share it, and any stop the
    cycle or those checks set is reported instead of STEP_PAUSED. currentStep
    advances only in endStep, so `currentStep > 1` means this turn finished a
    cycle. AC1b (parallel reads, v1.1.3 822c08fb) still pauses after one cycle.

    Behaviour changes: in step mode the empty-reply, notice, truncated-reply,
    capacity-retry, queued-steering and requirements-nudge continuations now
    also pause after their cycle instead of issuing another model request in
    the same /step; a step budget or time limit reached in the same cycle is
    reported as its own reason rather than STEP_PAUSED.

    Tests (red on origin/main, green here): "the step pause keeps a
    STAGNATION_DETECTED set during the cycle (recovery exhausted)" and "steps
    without progress accumulate across /step calls until STAGNATION_DETECTED".

    Findings: 01M6KXHHESMA, 01M6NE024XMN (WP-1301).

    Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
